629 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1728 0 R
/E (Women are over-represented. Women make up 58% of the Assembly against 52% of the state, almost entirely a matter of who accepted: the panel as drawn was 54% female, and 71% of the women on it took their seat, against 51% of the men.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
630 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1729 0 R
/E (Democrats are over-represented, independents and Republicans under-represented. They are 57% of the Assembly against 46% of the state, with independents \(20% vs. 25%\) and Republicans \(22% vs. 28%\) both short. The drawn panel had this right, at 48% Democratic. Independents declined far more often than anyone else, 47% of those drawn took their seat, against 68% of Democrats and 67% of Republicans.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
631 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1730 0 R
/E (Higher-income residents are under-represented. Half the Assembly lives in a household earning $100,000 or more, against 57% of Connecticut adults.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
632 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1731 0 R
/E (Hispanic and urban residents are under-represented. Hispanic and Latino residents are 12.6% of the Assembly against 16.1% of the state, and urban residents 9.7% against 16.3%. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
633 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1732 0 R
/E (Taken together, the Assembly looks like Connecticut on race and region, comes within a few points on homeownership and children at home, and is more female, more Democratic, more suburban and somewhat less affluent than the state.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
