774 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 341 0 R
/E (Q1. What problem or issue related to the property tax is this proposal seeking to address?)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
775 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 341 0 R
/E (Connecticut's cities and towns depend on the property tax for most of their revenue. That reliance places a heavy burden on homeowners and local businesses and leaves municipalities with few ways to keep pace with rising costs.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
776 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 341 0 R
/E (Restaurants and hotels drive economic activity that benefits the whole state, but the costs of hosting that activity fall on the host community. Police, fire, emergency response, traffic management and road maintenance are all paid for by local property taxpayers, while the sales and occupancy taxes generated by visitors go to the state.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
777 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 341 0 R
/E (This problem has been recognized before. In 2019, the additional 1% tax on meals and beverages was originally proposed by the Finance, Revenue and Bonding Committee as a way to give cities and towns a new source of revenue. The tax was adopted in Public Act 19-117, but the revenue was directed to the General Fund. Proposals to share room occupancy tax revenue with host communities date back more than two decades, and the occupancy tax today provides no direct revenue to the municipalities where the lodging is located.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
778 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 341 0 R
/E (This proposal addresses that gap. It would give municipalities a predictable revenue source tied to the activity they host, dedicated to the public safety and public works services that visitors use, and would reduce the amount that must be raised through the property tax.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
