/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 342 0 R
/T (Why this is a balanced approach: The state retains the majority of the occupancy tax, and its half of the restaurant revenue supports tourism promotion that grows both tax bases over time. Municipalities gain a predictable revenue source tied to the economic activity they host. Visitors, not only residents, help pay for the local services they rely on. The five-year average municipal share is estimated at $74.4 million a year, taken from the state's monthly room occupancy tax data \(total tax of $135.8M rising to $175.9M\) for the past five fiscal years.)
/E (Why this is a balanced approach: The state retains the majority of the occupancy tax, and its half of the restaurant revenue supports tourism promotion that grows both tax bases over time. Municipalities gain a predictable revenue source tied to the economic activity they host. Visitors, not only residents, help pay for the local services they rely on. The five-year average municipal share is estimated at $74.4 million a year, taken from the state's monthly room occupancy tax data \(total tax of $135.8M rising to $175.9M\) for the past five fiscal years.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
