2561 0 obj
<<
/Title (Restaurant sales tax \(the 1% portion\): The revenue from the 1% portion of the sales tax on meals and beverages would be divided equally. Half would go to the State Tourism Fund, to keep investing in the visitor economy that generates this revenue. The other half would go to the municipality where the sale took place, dedicated to public safety and public works. This mirrors the meals tax provision of Substitute Senate Bill 2 \(2026 session, File No. 693\), which received a favorable report from the Finance, Revenue and Bonding Committee and was supported by the Connecticut Conference of Municipalities, the Connecticut Business and Industry Association, and the restaurant, hospitality and tourism industries.)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2531 0 R
/Next 2588 0 R
>>
endobj
2562 0 obj
<<
/Title (Why this is a balanced approach: The state retains the majority of the occupancy tax, and its half of the restaurant revenue supports tourism promotion that grows both tax bases over time. Municipalities gain a predictable revenue source tied to the economic activity they host. Visitors, not only residents, help pay for the local services they rely on. The five-year average municipal share is estimated at $74.4 million a year, taken from the state's monthly room occupancy tax data \(total tax of $135.8M rising to $175.9M\) for the past five fiscal years.)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2531 0 R
/Prev 2589 0 R
>>
endobj
2563 0 obj
<<
/Title (Cost to the state budget: The proposal would move roughly $200 million a year out of the General Fund, at a time when the Office of Fiscal Analysis projects shortfalls of $112 million to $232 million a year from FY 2028 to FY 2030. The state would need to absorb the change through revenue growth, spending adjustments or a phased schedule, and in a way consistent with the fiscal guardrails pledged to bondholders.)
/Dest [31 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2503 0 R
/Prev 2532 0 R
/Next 2590 0 R
>>
endobj
2564 0 obj
<<
/Title (The Assembly recommends distributing the funds based on where the revenue was collected. The municipal share of both taxes would go directly to the city or town where the meal was sold or the lodging is located, because that community provides the police, fire, emergency and road services that visitors use.)
/Dest [31 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2503 0 R
/Prev 2591 0 R
/First 2592 0 R
/Last 2593 0 R
/Count -2
/Next 2533 0 R
>>
